※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4373
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4373 LANG_RELRO_WRITTEN_AFTER_STARTUP
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4374 LANG_RELRO_DEFINED_HERE

※ A `@[relro]` variable is written only in the body of a constructor.  By the
※ time the startup function runs, the entry point has made it read-only, and
※ a write there would stop the program on a fault -- so it is refused here,
※ where it is written, and the note says which definition made it so.
※
※ Every way of writing is a write: assigning it, a field or an element of it,
※ handing it on as `&mut`, walking it with `&mut`, joining onto a list it is.
※ Reading it, and taking a `&` of it, are what the rest of the program does.

let std := ⎕import("std")

@[relro]
let seed: mut u64 = 1u64

@[constructor, impure]
fn prepare():
    seed ← 7u64

@[startup, impure]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let read: &u64 = &seed
    seed ← read⌖ + 1u64
    0u6
